## **Network Architecture**

| **Layer** | **Configuration** | **Compliance Role** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Internet uplink | Xfinity Business Gigabit Extra — $150/mo, static IP, commercial ToS | Eliminates ToS violation risk |
| Hardwired result | 935 Mbps down / 90 Mbps up / 15 ms latency — confirmed | Adequate for all Phase 1 workloads |
| Client VLANs | Dedicated VLAN per B2B client — UniFi managed switch | No inter-client traffic possible |
| Firewall rules | Block all inter-VLAN routing; no outbound internet from GPU containers | Data isolation enforced in hardware |
| TLS encryption | NGINX reverse proxy, Let's Encrypt cert, TLS 1.3 on all endpoints | HIPAA in-transit encryption |
| Management access | WireGuard VPN only — SSH key-auth, non-standard port, root disabled | Admin surface never public-facing |
| Inference engine | vLLM — OpenAI-compatible API, bearer token auth per client | Zero prompt persistence by default |

# **3. Compliance Packaging — The Competitive Moat**

The compliance architecture is not a cost center. It is the primary product differentiator and the justification for premium pricing. SMB clients in regulated industries cannot safely use public AI tools — your private node solves a real legal and regulatory problem.

| **The Heppner Ruling — February 17, 2026 (S.D.N.Y.)** A federal judge ruled that documents created using publicly available AI platforms (including consumer Claude, ChatGPT) are NOT protected by attorney-client privilege — because data was voluntarily disclosed to a third-party server. Attorney-client privilege was waived. This ruling is the single most powerful sales tool for law firm outreach. A private endpoint with a signed Data Processing Agreement eliminates this risk entirely. |
| --- |

## **Compliance Requirements by Target Vertical**

| **Vertical** | **Governing Standard** | **Your Obligation** | **Differentiator** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Law firms | ABA Model Rule 1.6, Heppner (2026) | Data Processing Agreement, zero-retention architecture, no third-party disclosure | Privilege preservation — data never leaves their network path |
| Medical / dental offices | HIPAA Security Rule, HTI-1 (2025) | Signed BAA, PHI encryption in transit + at rest, audit logs, breach notification SOP | PHI never reaches a public AI server — HIPAA safe harbor |
| Financial / mortgage / insurance | GLBA Safeguard Rules, FINRA/SEC | Data Processing Agreement, access controls, 90-day audit log retention | No NPI exposure to third-party AI vendors |
| General SMBs | Florida Digital Bill of Rights, CCPA | Data Processing Agreement, zero-retention policy disclosure | Cost savings vs cloud APIs + data privacy assurance |

## **Six-Layer Security Stack**

| **Layer** | **Controls Implemented** | **Standard Met** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 — Physical | Keyed deadbolt, IP camera (30-day retention), cable-locked chassis, CO2 extinguisher, heat detector, single-occupant access | HIPAA Physical Safeguards |
| 2 — Network isolation | Per-client VLAN, inter-VLAN firewall block, no outbound from GPU containers, separate management VLAN | HIPAA Technical Safeguards |
| 3 — Encryption | TLS 1.3 in transit (NGINX + Let's Encrypt), LUKS full-disk encryption at rest, no plaintext credential storage | HIPAA Encryption Standard |
| 4 — Access control | Per-client bearer tokens, quarterly rotation, rate limiting per client, Fail2ban, SSH key-only auth | HIPAA Access Control, Minimum Necessary |
| 5 — Audit logging | NGINX structured logs: timestamp, client ID, endpoint, response code, latency — no prompt content logged. Grafana Loki, 90-day retention, tamper-evident, monthly report to each client | HIPAA Audit Controls |
| 6 — Legal / contractual | BAA (healthcare), DPA (all clients), MSA with liability cap, AUP, written breach notification SOP (24-hr client notice, 72-hr report) | HIPAA, GLBA, Florida law |

## **Zero-Retention Architecture — Core Selling Point**

| **What happens to client data** | **Public AI (ChatGPT / Claude.ai)** | **Your Private Node** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Prompt sent to server | Leaves client network to third-party | **Stays within TLS tunnel to your node only** |
| Data stored after processing | Retained per vendor ToS | **Never stored — GPU memory only, purged on response** |
| Used for model training | Possible — depends on ToS tier | **Never — open-weight model, you control weights** |
| Third-party subpoena exposure | Yes — Heppner ruling (Feb 2026) | **None — no third party has access** |
| BAA available | Enterprise tier only, limited scope | **Yes — signed directly with you, full scope** |
| Florida data residency guaranteed | No | **Yes — hardware at Hollywood, FL address** |
| Monthly audit report to client | No | **Yes — metadata only, zero-incident confirmation** |

